1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:22,200
Purpose is not a prize reserved for exceptional people. It becomes visible when what matters, what the client can offer, who or what they care about, and how they live begin to align. Coaching replaces pressure to discover one perfect calling with careful evidence, honest constraints, meaningful experiments, and client-owned decisions.

2
00:00:22,550 --> 00:00:43,620
We begin by separating purpose from achievement, role, income, and performance. Then we gather clues from values, strengths, energy, concern, contribution, relationships, and season. Finally, we organize those clues, design experiments, address common obstacles, and apply the process to Naomi's case.

3
00:00:43,970 --> 00:01:03,910
Purpose can be understood as a continuing direction of meaningful participation. It is how the client chooses to embody values, use strengths, care for people or causes, and meet the responsibilities of a particular season. Because life changes, purpose can change form without becoming false.

4
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:26,820
Purpose pressure tells clients there is one correct path, that work must provide every form of meaning, that aligned living should feel constantly inspiring, or that purpose must be publicly impressive. These myths produce paralysis and comparison. A useful direction can be ordinary, seasonal, difficult, private, and still deeply meaningful.

5
00:01:27,170 --> 00:01:46,950
A purpose statement means little if it never reaches behavior. Daily alignment appears in attention, boundaries, workmanship, relationships, service, learning, and recovery. The coach asks how a value would become observable this week. Direction grows through repeated choices, not slogans.

6
00:01:47,300 --> 00:01:57,840
Pause here and choose the response that best preserves client dignity, ownership, and coaching scope. Which statement best reflects the Roux Life view of purpose?

7
00:01:58,190 --> 00:02:19,650
The same value can take different forms. Contribution may look like study in one season, parenting in another, leadership later, and mentoring after that. Clients often think a former purpose disappeared when its expression no longer fits capacity. Coaching helps distinguish the enduring value from the current strategy.

8
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:40,060
Achievement answers whether a goal was reached. Meaning asks why it mattered and what kind of person the client was while pursuing it. A client may be proud and disconnected, or deeply fulfilled without public recognition. Coaches do not dismiss ambition; they help clients examine what achievement serves.

9
00:02:40,410 --> 00:02:59,830
A role can be a powerful vehicle for purpose, but it is not the whole person. If meaning depends on one title, any job change can feel like identity erasure. The client can locate purpose across work, family, friendship, citizenship, faith, creativity, learning, and self-stewardship.

10
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:21,400
Values are not outcomes. They are qualities the client chooses to practice: honesty, compassion, excellence, freedom, faith, curiosity, or justice. A value becomes useful when translated into behavior. ‘Connection’ might mean one present conversation; ‘courage’ might mean submitting work before certainty.

11
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:45,290
Strengths include more than talent. They may be learned skills, ways of thinking, relational capacities, or wisdom built through experience. The coach asks for evidence: when has the client organized complexity, calmed a room, taught clearly, repaired trust, designed beauty, or persisted responsibly? Strength is not permission for endless unpaid labor.

12
00:03:45,640 --> 00:03:58,720
Pause here and choose the response that best preserves client dignity, ownership, and coaching scope. A client says, ‘I lost my job, so I lost my purpose.’ What is the strongest question?

13
00:03:59,070 --> 00:04:24,650
Energy patterns reveal fit, but meaningful work can also be tiring. The coach looks beyond whether an activity feels easy. Does it create absorbed attention, satisfying effort, sustained curiosity, or healthy pride? What drains because it violates values, exceeds capacity, or lacks support? Clinical fatigue or medical concerns require appropriate evaluation.

14
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:46,310
Some clients repeatedly notice excluded people, broken systems, overlooked beauty, family patterns, education gaps, or community needs. Recurrence can be a direction clue. It is not a command to solve everything. The client chooses which concern belongs to them, in what form, and at what sustainable scale.

15
00:04:46,660 --> 00:05:07,900
Contribution can create meaning when a real strength meets a real need. It becomes unhealthy when worth depends on rescue, exhaustion, or being indispensable. The coach helps the client identify the contribution, recipient, consent, boundaries, and cost. Purpose should not require chronic self-abandonment.

16
00:05:08,250 --> 00:05:28,050
Vague purpose language becomes clearer when the client names who or what benefits. They may support first-generation students, strengthen their family, preserve land, create trustworthy systems, teach emerging leaders, or make a home where people feel welcome. Specificity makes experiments possible.

17
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:50,820
A direction that ignores health, money, caregiving, discrimination, location, access, or time can become shame disguised as inspiration. Constraints do not define the client's worth. They shape the present strategy. The coach helps distinguish fixed limits, negotiable conditions, needed support, and assumptions that deserve testing.

18
00:05:51,170 --> 00:06:00,550
Pause here and choose the response that best preserves client dignity, ownership, and coaching scope. Which purpose plan is most sustainable?

19
00:06:00,900 --> 00:06:23,050
The Roux purpose map brings five forms of evidence together. Values set direction. Strengths identify available capacity. Contribution names benefit. Season asks what life currently requires. Constraints shape a realistic expression. The intersection suggests experiments—not a destiny the coach declares.

20
00:06:23,400 --> 00:06:46,080
For several weeks, the client records moments of vitality, usefulness, integrity, belonging, absorption, or healthy pride. They note the activity, people, value, strength, energy before and after, and cost. Patterns are more reliable than one inspirational afternoon. Evidence replaces pressure to invent a perfect answer in session.

21
00:06:46,430 --> 00:07:06,900
Peak moments can reveal strengths and values. Low moments can reveal missing autonomy, connection, fairness, challenge, or support. The coach avoids assuming every low point requires leaving. Sometimes the role is wrong; sometimes the conditions, workload, boundaries, or strategy need adjustment.

22
00:07:07,250 --> 00:07:26,430
Envy often arrives as shame, but it can be examined. Is the client longing for creative practice, autonomy, courage, flexibility, expertise, contribution, or applause? The answer matters. The coach helps extract the value or condition without copying another person's life.

23
00:07:26,780 --> 00:07:49,220
Anger may signal a violated value; grief may reveal deep attachment. Neither emotion automatically defines purpose or authorizes action. The coach helps the client name what matters, gather evidence, and decide a proportionate response. Severe or persistent symptoms, trauma, or impairment require licensed care.

24
00:07:49,570 --> 00:08:02,430
Pause here and choose the response that best preserves client dignity, ownership, and coaching scope. A client envies a friend's flexible creative career. What should the coach explore first?

25
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:24,190
Dreaming expands possibility; fantasy ignores evidence and cost. The coach does not crush imagination or sell certainty. The client describes the desired experience, identifies assumptions, researches the actual conditions, and tests a small version. Better evidence creates better decisions.

26
00:08:24,540 --> 00:08:45,540
A client can gain direction without resigning tomorrow. They can shadow, volunteer, teach a workshop, join a board, publish a small piece, or change one responsibility. Each experiment has a question, time boundary, observation plan, and review. The result can be yes, no, not now, or a revised direction.

27
00:08:45,890 --> 00:09:05,620
A ninety-day theme such as ‘create visibly,’ ‘deepen community,’ or ‘lead sustainably’ focuses action without pretending permanence. The client chooses two weekly practices, one experiment, support, boundaries, and measures. After ninety days, they review evidence and decide what continues.

28
00:09:05,970 --> 00:09:27,520
If the calendar contains no space for the stated direction, the plan is aspirational rather than operational. The client audits obligations, identifies what can stop, start, continue, delegate, or renegotiate, and protects a realistic block. Recovery belongs in an aligned life; it is not wasted time.

29
00:09:27,870 --> 00:09:48,010
A client may live deep purpose through caregiving, friendship, craftsmanship, hospitality, stewardship, learning, faith, or citizenship. Coaches should not rank entrepreneurial visibility above ordinary contribution. The client decides what makes life meaningful, and that meaning may remain private.

30
00:09:48,360 --> 00:09:59,710
Pause here and choose the response that best preserves client dignity, ownership, and coaching scope. Which is the best next step for a client considering a teaching career?

31
00:10:00,060 --> 00:10:21,950
Direction becomes clearer in relationship. Trusted people can reflect strengths, offer reality, open access, challenge overreach, and receive contribution. The coach helps the client identify healthy communities and make specific connection requests. Belonging should not require abandoning values or tolerating harm.

32
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:42,600
Clients often wait to feel completely sure. Direction needs less certainty than a final answer. A chosen value and reversible next step can guide movement while information is incomplete. The client reviews evidence and adjusts. Revision is not failure; it is responsible navigation.

33
00:10:42,950 --> 00:11:03,040
Thoughtful preparation has a decision point. Perfectionism keeps adding conditions so the work never becomes visible. The coach helps define ‘ready enough,’ reduce the experiment, identify the feared consequence, and choose support. Clinical anxiety or severe impairment is outside coaching scope.

34
00:11:03,390 --> 00:11:24,820
Some clients want impact but fear being seen; others feel pressured to build a public brand. Visibility is a strategy, not a moral requirement. The client decides what exposure serves the purpose, what privacy protects, and how to tolerate appropriate vulnerability without oversharing or abandoning boundaries.

35
00:11:25,170 --> 00:11:44,440
Comparison removes context. Two people may have different money, health, support, discrimination, caregiving, timing, or appetite for risk. The coach helps the client learn from another person's strategy while returning to their own map. Direction should fit the client's actual life.

36
00:11:44,790 --> 00:12:07,130
Clients may monetize meaningful work, keep it separate from income, or blend the two. No option is automatically superior. Turning every gift into a business can damage joy; refusing financial reality can create harm. A life may carry several purposes across work, relationship, health, creativity, and community.

37
00:12:07,480 --> 00:12:21,230
Pause here and choose the response that best preserves client dignity, ownership, and coaching scope. A coach believes Naomi should leave her job to pursue meaningful work. What is the ethical response?

38
00:12:21,580 --> 00:12:53,600
Naomi is respected at work and devoted to family, but feels she is only completing tasks. She assumes purpose requires resignation and one extraordinary calling. Her map reveals values of creativity, mentorship, and family presence; strengths in teaching and design; limited weekday capacity; and energy when helping younger colleagues. She chooses a ninety-day experiment: one monthly mentoring circle, two protected creative blocks, and a calendar audit.

39
00:12:53,950 --> 00:13:22,250
Review vitality, values alignment, contribution, boundaries, relationships, health, and practical consequences. If emptiness includes persistent hopelessness, major loss of interest, severe fatigue, impairment, self-harm, trauma symptoms, substance concerns, or possible medical causes, ordinary purpose coaching is not enough. Refer to qualified professionals and respond immediately to safety concerns.

40
00:13:22,600 --> 00:13:48,570
Roux Life coaching does not declare a client's calling or borrow another teacher's branded curriculum. We draw on broad themes of courage, values, empathy, belonging, boundaries, and resilience through original Roux tools. Purpose is client-defined, evidence-informed, season-aware, and translated into sustainable action—with referral when clinical, medical, or safety needs exceed coaching scope.
